.SH DESCRIPTION
.B yume
is the native YUME Transport Protocol 1 client.
It reads a schema-1 client configuration, maintains an authenticated
session to
.BR yumed (8),
and runs the configured SOCKS5 and forward listeners.
SOCKS5 CONNECT carries TCP streams; UDP ASSOCIATE carries datagrams
through a configured packet service.
A forward turns each connection to its loopback port or UNIX socket into
a stream on its service, optionally to a fixed destination.
The server enforces each service's destination policy.
.PP
A Linux packet adapter connects a named service to a managed IP/TUN
interface and owns its addresses, routes and optional per-link DNS.
The client is not yet qualified for production.
Check
.B docs/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md
before deployment.
.PP
With a
.B circuits
section, every SOCKS5 CONNECT and every forward to a destination leaves
through a circuit of two or three servers of the entry's cluster, the
entry being the configured server.
The client fetches the operator-signed routes view from the entry,
chooses the other servers and builds the circuit.
UDP ASSOCIATE is refused and a packet adapter is not allowed, since
circuits carry TCP only, and plain HTTP is refused unless
.B circuits.plain_http
is
.BR allow .
When no route of
.B circuits.hops
can be built, the client does not shorten it on its own: connections are
refused as not allowed until the user accepts its proposal with
.BR --accept-route ,
unless
.B circuits.min_hops
approves the shorter length in advance.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI "--config " path
Read exactly one schema-1 JSON configuration.
Its role must match the program: client for
.BR yume ,
server for
.BR yumed .
Required unless
.B --help
or
.B --version
is requested.
Security policy has no command-line overrides.
.TP
.B --validate
Check schema, role, referenced credentials, direct-adapter destination
policy and the daemon's module programs, then exit without opening a
listener or network connection.
This checks local inputs; it does not test connectivity or complete
runtime startup, including loading the cover site, matching services to
runtime adapters, TUN privileges or DNS authorization.
.TP
.B --version
Print the product version, transport version, configuration schema,
suite, session-security provider, crypto backend and evidence profile.
This operation uses local information and does not read a configuration
or contact a server.
.TP
.BI "--completion " shell
Print the completion script for
.IR shell ,
which must be
.BR bash ,
and exit.
The script completes this program's options, their fixed values and file
arguments.
To load it for one user, save it under the program's name in
.IR ~/\:.local/\:share/\:bash-completion/\:completions/ .
This operation reads no configuration.
.TP
.B -h, --help
Print the supported options and exit.
Unknown arguments still fail parsing when combined with
.BR --help ,
.B --version
or
.BR --completion .
.PP
Each run setting replaces one configuration key for this run only and
passes the same checks as the file's value.
An error names the key's JSON pointer and says that the value came from
the command line.
No run setting can change the wire image, authentication, encryption,
admission, destination policy or leak protection.
.B --validate
prints every key a run setting can replace, with its value and whether
it came from the configuration or a flag.
.TP
.BI "--connect " ip
Dial this IP literal instead of resolving the configured host, as
.B endpoint.connect_address
does.
The TLS server name stays the configured host, and TLS and admission
still authenticate it.
Through a configured SOCKS5 upstream proxy, the proxy connects to this
address.
.TP
.BI "--socks-address " ip
Set the listen address of the configuration's only SOCKS5 adapter.
The configuration's rule applies: 127.0.0.1 or ::1.
.TP
.BI "--socks-port " port
Set the listen port of the configuration's only SOCKS5 adapter, 1 to
65535.
.TP
.BI "--control-socket " path
Serve the control socket at
.I path
instead of
.BR control.socket ,
or add one to a configuration without it, so a program that starts
.BR yume ,
such as the desktop GUI, chooses where to find it.
The path must be absolute and normalized, in a directory this user owns
that is not group or world writable.
With
.B --status
or
.B --accept-route
it names the socket to ask.
.PP
The status option asks the running client of the same configuration for
its state.
.TP
.B --status
Connect to the control socket that
.B control.socket
or
.B --control-socket
names, print the running client's state and exit.
The report names the connection state, how long it has lasted or when
the next attempt starts, the server and its verified identity, the
sessions and failed attempts, payload and record bytes in each
direction, the local listeners and the last failure.
The exit status is 2 when neither names a control socket and 1 when no
client answers on it.
.B --status
takes no option besides
.B --config
and
.BR --control-socket .
With circuits, it also shows each circuit's route by name, the hop count
in use against the configured one, whether plain HTTP is refused, and
any proposal of a shorter route with its id and what that route gives
up.
.TP
.BI "--accept-route " id
Accept the shorter route that the running client of this configuration
proposed, when no route of
.B circuits.hops
could be built.
The
.I id
is the one
.B --status
shows, so an answer to an older proposal accepts nothing.
The acceptance lasts until routes of the configured length work again or
the client exits.
Only a process of the client's own user can send it.
Exit statuses follow
.BR --status ,
and an id no proposal has exits with status 2.
.PP
The kit options move a client kit to another device in one file, a
sealed kit.
Each pair stands alone.
.TP
.BI "--seal-kit " directory
Seal the client kit in
.I directory
into the file that
.B --output
names, and print a new code in five groups of five characters.
The kit is the directory's regular files and those one level below it,
at most 32 files of up to 256 KiB each, and its
.I yume.json
must be a client configuration.
The file is Argon2id and AES-256-GCM output with no plaintext header, so
it reads as random bytes.
Pass the code to the user by another channel than the file.
.TP
.BI "--output " file
Write the sealed kit to
.I file
with mode 0600.
An existing file is not replaced.
.TP
.BI "--import-kit " file
Open the sealed kit in
.I file
with the code read from standard input, echo off on a terminal, and
write it to the new directory that
.B --into
names.
Case, dashes and spaces in the code do not matter.
A wrong code and a file that is not a sealed kit fail the same way.
The directory is written under a private temporary name beside it and
renamed into place, with mode 0700 for directories and programs and 0600
for other files.
It must not exist.
.TP
.BI "--into " directory
The directory
.B --import-kit
creates.
Its parent must exist.
.PP
The evidence option observes the run and changes nothing on the wire.
.TP
.BI "--outer-carrier-evidence " path
When the run ends, write a report on the first connection's HTTP/2 and
WebSocket carrier to
.IR path ,
for comparison with the captured browser session.
The report holds frame types, sizes, header names with pinned or
redacted values, and timing.
It holds no payload, key, address or admission value.
.I path
must be absolute, in a directory this user owns that is not group or
world writable and not inside a Git worktree, and must not exist.
The file is created with mode 0600 when the run starts.
The exit status is 1 when the report is incomplete, for example when the
session never closed gracefully.
.B --validate
does not accept this option.
.SH CONFIGURATION
The configuration owns endpoint addresses, exact suite, credential file
references, cover profile, services, adapters and resource limits, and
optionally a control socket, a server's cluster membership and a
client's circuits.
The schema rejects unknown fields, wrong types and unsupported values.
Credential paths are relative to the configuration file's directory
unless absolute.
Credential loading rejects unsafe file types and permissions; inline
secrets are forbidden.
.SH CONTROL SOCKET
With
.B control.socket
set, the running program listens on that UNIX socket for processes of
its own user.
The directory must belong to that user and be closed to writes by group
and others, the socket is mode 0600, and a connection from another user
is closed.
At most four connections are open at once.
.PP
A connection sends one line, the JSON object
.BR {"control":1,"request":"status"} ,
within two seconds and in at most 512 bytes.
The program answers with one JSON object on one line and closes the
connection, and
.B --status
refuses an answer over 64 KiB.
A request that fails gets an object that holds
.BR control ,
an
.B error
text and a
.BR code .
No answer holds a key, credential or payload.
.B docs/protocol/CONTROL_1.md
is the protocol's contract: every request, every field of both programs'
answers, and the rules by which later versions add fields and requests
without breaking older clients.
.PP
The client's answer holds its connection
.BR state ,
the server, traffic totals, its local listeners, the last failure, its
fixed posture and tuning limits and, with a
.B circuits
section, its routes and any proposal of a shorter route.
The client with circuits also takes the request
{"control":\:1,\:"request":\:"accept-route",\:"id":\:ID}, which
.B --accept-route
sends.
Both programs take
{"control":\:1,\:"request":\:"messages",\:"after":\:N}, which returns
the lines they printed after line
.IR N ,
and the client takes {"control":1,"request":"stop"}, which stops it as
.B SIGTERM
does.
.SH SIGNALS
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
request shutdown of listeners and sessions, followed by removal of owned
TUN networking.
The session closes its WebSocket the way the captured browser does and
waits up to three seconds for the server's answer before it ends the
connection.
.B SIGPIPE
is ignored.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates successful help, version or validation output, or
shutdown requested by a handled signal with successful cleanup.
Exit status 1 indicates a runtime or internal failure.
Exit status 2 indicates invalid arguments, configuration or startup
prerequisites.
Diagnostics go to standard error; help and version output go to standard
output.
Argument errors also print usage on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Validate an existing client kit, then start its configured listeners:
.PP
.nf
yume --config /path/to/client.json --validate
yume --config /path/to/client.json
.fi
.PP
The separately installed
.B yume-setup
and
.B yume-doctor
tools provision and inspect schema-1 kits.
Their
.B --help
output describes their arguments.
Setup and doctor are separate programs.
